  "sections": {
    "BASICS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Caused by the zoonotic rickettsiaCoxiella burnetii.",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Infection—most commonly by inhalation or ingestion of organism via infected body fluids (parturient discharges, urine, milk, or feces), tissues (especially placenta), or carcasses of infected animal reservoir hosts (cattle, sheep, goats); can occur after tick exposure (many species of ticks implicated).",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Lungs—thought to be main portal of entry to systemic circulation.",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Organism replicates in vascular endothelium; causes widespread vasculitis; vasculitis results in necrosis and hemorrhage in lungs, liver, and CNS; severity depends on the pathogenicity of the strain of organism and host immune response.",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "An extended latent period exists after recovery until chronic immune-complex phenomena develop; organism reactivated out of the latent state during parturition, resulting in large pathogen load in placenta, parturient fluids, urine, feces, and milk.",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Endemic in mammals worldwide; most cases in the United States in western states.",
              "pdf_page": 1258,
              "printed_page": 1176,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
